# Cloudflare Pages headers. Applied at the edge from the ROOT of the uploaded
# directory only — deploy.yml assembles `_site/` from prod-src/site/, so this
# file is the root one and its rules cover the /dev/ lane too. (A copy also
# lands at _site/dev/_headers from main; Cloudflare ignores nested ones.)
# GitHub Pages ignores this file entirely, which is fine — it is on its way out.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# On 2026-08-10 account-modal.js was shipped with a syntax error that broke the
# community and clubs pages. The fix was deployed and verified with curl — and
# the browser kept running the broken file anyway.
#
# The default here was `Cache-Control: public, max-age=14400`: a FOUR HOUR
# browser cache. `must-revalidate` does not help, because it only applies once
# max-age has expired; inside the window the browser never asks. So a corrected
# file was invisible for four hours to every device that had already loaded the
# broken one, and the site looked unfixed long after it was fixed.
#
# ⚠️ THE KEY FACT: none of this site's assets are content-hashed. `identity.js`,
# `account-modal.js`, `reviews.js` and the JSON payloads all keep the same
# filename forever and change their bytes in place. That is the exact opposite
# of the situation where a long cache is safe. `immutable`, and the year-long
# max-age used for hashed bundles in library_catalog, would be a lie here — the
# URL genuinely does not change when the content does.
#
# So: never trust a cached copy of anything whose name is stable.
#
# The cost is one conditional request per file per load. Cloudflare serves these
# with an ETag, so an unchanged file answers 304 with no body. That is a
# round trip, not a download, and it buys the guarantee that a deploy is a
# deploy rather than a suggestion.
#
# Covers are deliberately NOT listed. They are large, one per book, and replaced
# almost never — they keep the default and should. This file is about
# correctness of code and data, not about image bandwidth.
#
# If hashed filenames ever arrive (a build step, a bundler), THOSE may be
# `immutable` with a long max-age, and should be — see
# library_catalog/apps/web/public/_headers, which does exactly that because Vite
# hashes its bundles.

# ⚠️ Extension patterns like `/*.js` DO NOT WORK here. The first version of this
# file used them and measured no effect: identity.js still came back
# `max-age=14400` after deploying. Cloudflare's `*` is a path splat, not a glob,
# so a wildcard has to be a trailing segment. `/*` and `/covers/*` are the shapes
# that match. (The `no-cache` seen on .html was Pages' own default for HTML, not
# a rule of ours — easy to misread as the pattern working.)
#
# So: catch everything, then hand the images their cache back. Later rules win
# for the same header name, which is why the override sits below the catch-all
# and not above it. A new .js file added tomorrow is covered without anyone
# remembering to list it, which an explicit filename list would not manage.

/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/covers/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/dev/covers/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800

/static/img/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800
